{"id": "hallu-dch-subject-swap", "label": "hallucinated", "claim": "The lipid nanoparticle formulation preserved 95 percent of mRNA integrity after 30 days at 4 C.", "cited_chunk_ids": ["c-mrna-1"], "chunk_texts": {"c-mrna-1": "After 30 days of storage at 4 C the lipid nanoparticle formulation retained 95 percent of siRNA integrity as measured by capillary electrophoresis."}, "note": "DENSELY-CITED-HALLUCINATION (subject-swap): every token and number in the claim appears in the evidence, BUT the evidence is about siRNA, not mRNA. Same polarity, no fabricated numbers, near-perfect coverage — yet the claim is about the wrong molecule. A pure token-overlap scorer scores this HIGH (blind to the entity distinction)."}
{"id": "hallu-dch-cause-inversion", "label": "hallucinated", "claim": "The optothermal Raman method increased the thermal conductivity of the graphene sample to 5000 W per meter per Kelvin at room temperature.", "cited_chunk_ids": ["c-graphene-1"], "chunk_texts": {"c-graphene-1": "At room temperature the suspended graphene sample exhibited a thermal conductivity of 5000 W per meter per Kelvin measured by the optothermal Raman method."}, "note": "DENSELY-CITED-HALLUCINATION (measurement-method misattributed as cause): all tokens/numbers identical, no negation, no fabricated numerals. The falsehood is a relation error: the chunk says Raman MEASURED the conductivity; the claim says Raman INCREASED it. Honest caveat: this is 'true-but-misleading' (the number and method are both real) rather than flatly false — a generous reader might excuse it. Kept because the relation error is exactly what an entailment backend (SPR-02) should catch and a lexical proxy cannot."}
{"id": "hallu-dch-correlation-causation", "label": "hallucinated", "claim": "The 75 kWh battery pack causes the electric vehicle to achieve a WLTP range of 600 km on a single charge.", "cited_chunk_ids": ["c-ev-1"], "chunk_texts": {"c-ev-1": "On a single charge of its 75 kWh battery pack the electric vehicle achieved a WLTP range of 600 km."}, "note": "DENSELY-CITED-HALLUCINATION (correlation-to-causation lift): every token and number is faithfully drawn from the evidence and polarity matches, but the claim asserts a CAUSAL relation (the pack CAUSES the range) that the evidence only states as an observed correlation. A common LLM synthesis slip — turning 'achieved X with Y' into 'Y causes X'. Token-overlap cannot detect the unwarranted causal lift."}
{"id": "hallu-dch-superlative", "label": "hallucinated", "claim": "The perovskite solar cell reached the highest power conversion efficiency ever recorded, at 26.1 percent under standard AM1.5 illumination.", "cited_chunk_ids": ["c-pero-1"], "chunk_texts": {"c-pero-1": "Under standard AM1.5 illumination the perovskite solar cell reached a certified power conversion efficiency of 26.1 percent, calibrated against a reference cell at the national test center."}, "note": "DENSELY-CITED-HALLUCINATION (unsupported-superlative): the evidence says 'a certified efficiency of 26.1 percent' — it does NOT support the claim that this is the highest EVER recorded. Every other token/number matches; the unsupported superlative ('highest ever recorded') is not detectable by overlap. Same polarity, no fabricated numbers."}
{"id": "hallu-dch-temporal-shift", "label": "hallucinated", "claim": "The titanium alloy exhibited a yield strength of 950 MPa after heat treatment at 700 C for 2 hours.", "cited_chunk_ids": ["c-alloy-1"], "chunk_texts": {"c-alloy-1": "Heat treatment at 700 C for 2 hours increased the yield strength of the titanium alloy to 950 MPa before eventual fracture at 880 MPa."}, "note": "DENSELY-CITED-HALLUCINATION (dropped temporal qualifier): all numbers/polarity identical. The chunk states 950 MPa is a PEAK before fracture drops it to 880 MPa; the claim states 950 MPa as a durable property, dropping the fracture qualifier. Honest caveat: 'true-but-misleading' (950 MPa was genuinely reached) rather than flatly false. Kept because dropping the failure qualifier materially changes the engineering claim and a lexical proxy cannot see the omission."}
{"id": "hallu-dch-unit-ambiguity", "label": "hallucinated", "claim": "The superconducting qubit maintained a T2 coherence time of 100 milliseconds in the dilution refrigerator at 15 millikelvin.", "cited_chunk_ids": ["c-qubit-1"], "chunk_texts": {"c-qubit-1": "In the dilution refrigerator at a base temperature of 15 millikelvin the superconducting qubit showed a T2 coherence time of 100 microseconds."}, "note": "DENSELY-CITED-HALLUCINATION (unit-class swap, same stem): claim says 100 MILLISECONDS but evidence says 100 MICROSECONDS — a 1000x error. The number '100' appears in evidence so the fabricated-number gate does NOT fire, and 'milliseconds'/'microseconds' share the stem 'micro'/'milli' so token-overlap stays high. Note: the scorer's number regex sees only the bare '100', so this is engineered to slip BOTH gates. (This is the strongest blind-spot case — a real model often confuses these stems.)"}
{"id": "hallu-dch-aggregation-error", "label": "hallucinated", "claim": "Each of the 100 offshore wind turbines generates 1200 MW of nameplate capacity.", "cited_chunk_ids": ["c-wind-1"], "chunk_texts": {"c-wind-1": "The offshore wind farm comprises 100 turbines with a combined nameplate capacity of 1200 MW."}, "note": "DENSELY-CITED-HALLUCINATION (aggregation/distribution error): the evidence says 1200 MW is the COMBINED capacity of all 100 turbines; the claim asserts EACH turbine is 1200 MW (a 100x error). Numbers 100 and 1200 are both present in evidence so the number-gate does not fire; no negation. Pure overlap scores this HIGH — the aggregation error is invisible to it."}
